os}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305051 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "637b9489-5e79-57b1-94e5-7750430c8469" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303059}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "637b9489-5e79-57b1-94e5-7750430c8469" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303059} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb1569c7-744b-5760-b694-df5b6e935708" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304586} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b6c1d08d-5dfb-5267-94f6-a91218062279" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303588}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b6c1d08d-5dfb-5267-94f6-a91218062279" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303588} :answer-field/id #uuid "e01b2515-1c42-50e8-b9ca-9ce208bad53a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304685}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "54143b7e-cf85-50bc-aa52-bc77e56d6283" :question/math-academy-id "q-332529" :question/problem "Express $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{11\\pi }{6})$ in terms of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\n$\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{11\\pi }{6})=$\n{{field-1}}$\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,+$\n{{field-2}}$\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for cosine with $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{11\\pi }{6}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{11\\pi }{6}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{6})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305115 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "afdd6f8e-0b6b-514c-a915-aa2e8e29eb4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{26}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303541}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "afdd6f8e-0b6b-514c-a915-aa2e8e29eb4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{26}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303541} :answer-field/id #uuid "095920a0-e5b5-514e-82f3-c5ad9b6c28af" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304089} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4fe287be-e149-5587-a57e-f34b2a95a19b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302956}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4fe287be-e149-5587-a57e-f34b2a95a19b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302956} :answer-field/id #uuid "6db84626-7422-5ccb-9647-85f889615f84" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304375}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "25c57c7f-1052-5419-977a-cd6a58624ada" :question/math-academy-id "q-332777" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n6\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-12y=9\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{2x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=A{e}^{2x}\\,+\\,($\n{{field-1}}\n$)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,+\\,($\n{{field-2}}\n$)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)$" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n6\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}-12{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $9\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x).$ So, we assume the particular solution to be \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n6(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))-12(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))=9\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(18\\beta -12\\alpha )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+(-18\\alpha -12\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)=9\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}18\\beta -12\\alpha &=9\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)) \\\\ -18\\alpha -12\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{3}{13},\\beta =\\frac{9}{26}.$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{3}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+\\frac{9}{26}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{2x}+(-\\frac{3}{13})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+(\\frac{9}{26})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305116 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "261d1aee-6819-5e96-bd40-2a6393b57a49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302703}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "261d1aee-6819-5e96-bd40-2a6393b57a49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302703} :answer-field/id #uuid "2acea709-491d-5884-b668-bfbe2b38b5f0" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304175} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f23efac-bf0b-5cee-92ab-f1da70d19d55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{6}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303347}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f23efac-bf0b-5cee-92ab-f1da70d19d55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{6}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303347} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f4c4077-795e-5834-bca9-23276a242c59" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304250}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cfad56c9-bef9-5eed-8564-b727d49c6b22" :question/math-academy-id "q-332778" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-9y=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x).\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{3x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=A{e}^{3x}\\,+\\,($\n{{field-1}}\n$)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\,+\\,($\n{{field-2}}\n$)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)$" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n3\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}-9{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x).$ So, we assume the particular solution to be \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n3(-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x))-9(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x))=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x).\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(12\\beta -9\\alpha )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+(-12\\alpha -9\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x).\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}12\\beta -9\\alpha &=6\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)) \\\\ -12\\alpha -9\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{6}{25},\\beta =\\frac{8}{25}.$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{6}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+\\frac{8}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x).\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{3x}+(-\\frac{6}{25})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+(\\frac{8}{25})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305119 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7f80fb1b-d38e-5a24-8d23-a1d10021010e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{-{s}^{2}+s+10}{s(s+1)(s-4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303251} {:answer/id #uuid "f9672cef-bf4e-59e9-b645-aca152e48467" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{-s^{2}+s+10}{s(s+1)(s-4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434029}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f9672cef-bf4e-59e9-b645-aca152e48467" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{-s^{2}+s+10}{s(s+1)(s-4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434029} :answer-field/id #uuid "8acdc256-05ee-570c-8605-17d181b69a3c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304448}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "53d3abf7-a9e9-5b69-8cf5-e63a711cb51d" :question/math-academy-id "q-332784" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=-{x}_{1}(t)+5 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=2{x}_{1}(t)+4{x}_{2}(t),\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform $L$ to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a system with variables ${X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}.$ In terms of the parameter $s,$ the solution of the new system is\n\n${X}_{1}(s)=\\frac{s+5}{s(s+1)},\\,$\n${X}_{2}(s)=$ {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the Laplace transforms of both sides of each equation in our system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}L\\{{x}_{1}^{′}(t)\\} &=L\\{-{x}_{1}(t)+5\\} \\\\ L\\{{x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\} &=L\\{2{x}_{1}(t)+4{x}_{2}(t)\\}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,\\,\n$$\n\nUsing the derivative property of the Laplace transform, we obtain the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}sL\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}-{x}_{1}(0) &=-L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}+5L\\{1\\} \\\\ sL\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}-{x}_{2}(0) &=2L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}+4L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhich can be simplified to the following:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{1}(s)-1 &=-{X}_{1}(s)+\\frac{5}{s} \\\\ s{X}_{2}(s)+1 &=2{X}_{1}(s)+4{X}_{2}(s)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere ${X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\},$ and we have used the fact that $L\\{1\\}=\\frac{1}{s}.$\n\nNow, solving the first equation for ${X}_{1}(s),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{1}(s)-1 & =-{X}_{1}(s)+\\frac{5}{s} \\\\ (s+1){X}_{1}(s) & =1+\\frac{5}{s} \\\\ (s+1){X}_{1}(s) & =\\frac{s+5}{s} \\\\ {X}_{1}(s) & =\\frac{s+5}{s(s+1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this in the second equation and solving for ${X}_{2}(s),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{2}(s)+1 & =2{X}_{1}(s)+4{X}_{2}(s) \\\\ (s-4){X}_{2}(s) & =2{X}_{1}(s)-1 \\\\ (s-4){X}_{2}(s) & =2(\\frac{s+5}{s(s+1)})-1 \\\\ (s-4){X}_{2}(s) & =\\frac{2(s+5)-s(s+1)}{s(s+1)} \\\\ (s-4){X}_{2}(s) & =\\frac{-{s}^{2}+s+10}{s(s+1)} \\\\ {X}_{2}(s) & =\\frac{-{s}^{2}+s+10}{s(s+1)(s-4)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution of the new system  in terms of parameter $s$ is \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}(s)=\\frac{s+5}{s(s+1)},\\,{X}_{2}(s)=\\frac{-{s}^{2}+s+10}{s(s+1)(s-4)}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305127 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a6f41bc-301b-535d-b2d9-d7659f80cca3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{15}{26}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302926}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a6f41bc-301b-535d-b2d9-d7659f80cca3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{15}{26}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302926} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c5b9454-0dc9-5101-863e-f86eb91ff515" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304451} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9b28912-7127-566d-af54-dbbde6b83cfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303503}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a9b28912-7127-566d-af54-dbbde6b83cfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303503} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f56ae57-1c8f-5446-b3fa-ba283d01d257" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304461}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "28e4dd91-b79c-54ce-a742-3311e6821446" :question/math-academy-id "q-332836" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-8y=10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{2x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=A{e}^{2x}\\,+\\,($\n{{field-1}}\n$)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,+\\,($\n{{field-2}}\n$)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)$" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n4\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}-8{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).$ So, we assume the particular solution to be \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n4(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))-8(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))=10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(12\\beta -8\\alpha )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+(-12\\alpha -8\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)=10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}12\\beta -8\\alpha &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)) \\\\ -12\\alpha -8\\beta &=10\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{15}{26},\\beta =-\\frac{5}{13}.$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{15}{26}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)-\\frac{5}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{2x}+(-\\frac{15}{26})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+(-\\frac{5}{13})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305128 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c4e3c2af-06c8-5299-8b7b-d4f60c7354e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303667}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c4e3c2af-06c8-5299-8b7b-d4f60c7354e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303667} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1e607dd-fd75-581f-baca-c306ad611ebe" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304515} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aefa0f5d-2538-5b17-9e6c-00e58297498c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303534}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aefa0f5d-2538-5b17-9e6c-00e58297498c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303534} :answer-field/id #uuid "d629bb55-4142-5ac1-b9df-8974a4b1bb66" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304661}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7750ffc9-4d2d-5351-8a60-0bfee62f5827" :question/math-academy-id "q-332845" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n7\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+14y=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-2x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=A{e}^{-2x}\\,+\\,($\n{{field-1}}\n$)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,+\\,($\n{{field-2}}\n$)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n7\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}+14{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).$ So, we assume the particular solution to be \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n7(-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))+14(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(14\\alpha +14\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+(-14\\alpha +14\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}14\\alpha +14\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)) \\\\ -14\\alpha +14\\beta &=-4\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{1}{7},\\beta =-\\frac{1}{7}.$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{1}{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-\\frac{1}{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-2x}+(\\frac{1}{7})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+(-\\frac{1}{7})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305129 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e6816377-a282-59e1-96a1-6f437967c002" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{32}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303888}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e6816377-a282-59e1-96a1-6f437967c002" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{32}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303888} :answer-field/id #uuid "831a99b5-5908-55e6-99d9-125b2e0da474" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304432} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6ae7ffdf-2c88-5c1a-8643-dfdd21761774" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{32}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303110}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6ae7ffdf-2c88-5c1a-8643-dfdd21761774" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{32}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303110} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4e47e09-154d-54ae-94df-564e8752ae2b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304613}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8124ea35-df1c-5551-a88f-dffd83f92db2" :question/math-academy-id "q-332847" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+16y=9\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x).\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-4x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=A{e}^{-4x}\\,+\\,($\n{{field-1}}\n$)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\,+\\,($\n{{field-2}}\n$)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)$" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n4\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}+16{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-4x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $9\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x).$ So, we assume the particular solution to be \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x).\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n4(-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x))+16(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x))=9\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x).\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(16\\alpha +16\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+(-16\\alpha +16\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)=9\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x).\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}16\\alpha +16\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)) \\\\ -16\\alpha +16\\beta &=9\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{9}{32},\\beta =\\frac{9}{32}.$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{9}{32}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+\\frac{9}{32}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x).\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-4x}+(-\\frac{9}{32})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+(\\frac{9}{32})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305131 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e7a68592-4e17-544c-b54a-9e28330e34ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303896}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e7a68592-4e17-544c-b54a-9e28330e34ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303896} :answer-field/id #uuid "243f4e92-ca09-501a-adea-b2badcc46a7b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304161} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aae1fdd1-9bc5-5a9b-9443-edc5f7f2ffc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303509}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aae1fdd1-9bc5-5a9b-9443-edc5f7f2ffc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303509} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d5fb19e-2f5b-58a5-8cc6-579a6fe3865d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304455}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "440cdd9d-8c3c-5d4a-8190-7594212042ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-332850" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n5\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+15y=-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-3x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=A{e}^{-3x}\\,+\\,($\n{{field-1}}\n$)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,+\\,($\n{{field-2}}\n$)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n5\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}+15{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).$ So, we assume the particular solution to be \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n5(-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))+15(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))=-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(10\\beta +15\\alpha )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+(-10\\alpha +15\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)=-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}10\\beta +15\\alpha &=-5\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)) \\\\ -10\\alpha +15\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{3}{13},\\beta =-\\frac{2}{13}.$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{3}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-\\frac{2}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-3x}+(-\\frac{3}{13})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+(-\\frac{2}{13})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305133 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3edc75cc-a655-5f39-a948-71fa367a6215" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302849} {:answer/id #uuid "603fc7c9-56aa-527d-9014-5e5a248674ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303042} {:answer/id #uuid "76bd3396-81a0-5e9c-a971-d928a196bb31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303184} {:answer/id #uuid "93ee005e-c0c3-5ed3-919a-ce496f2f1597" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303376} {:answer/id #uuid "a2351e4b-9c09-540d-ade1-8e32b50795d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303454}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a2351e4b-9c09-540d-ade1-8e32b50795d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303454} :answer-field/id #uuid "d54ba66f-0b9a-596e-b61e-f7c6549dfefe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304659}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e1a86a65-5d24-5f83-8695-88153c4cb0c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-332858" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x-y-1,\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton Method. What entry should be placed in the cell indicated in the table below, where the first step has already been computed using the Runge-Kutta method, if the step size is $Δx=1?$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\frac{7}{8}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n| $2$ | $\\,?\\,$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\n*Hint: The corrector equation of the two-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method is defined as*\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\n*where ${y}_{p}$ denotes the predictor at the current step, and ${y}_{-1}^{′}$ is the estimated slope at the previous point.*" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton (ABM2) method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{-1}^{′}=f({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1}),$ and\n\n- ${y}_{p}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nWe're told that the first step is calculated using the Runge-Kutta Method, resulting in \n\n$$\ny(1)≈\\frac{1}{8}.\n$$\n\nNow, we'll continue from this point, using the ABM $2$-step method.\n\nFirst, we compute the value of ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x-y-1 \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{8}-1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\frac{7}{8}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $-\\frac{1}{8}$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nFrom the first row of the table, we see that the slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})=(0,1)$ is ${y}_{-1}^{′}=-2.$\n\nNext, we compute $Δ{y}_{p}$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{8})-(-2))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\frac{7}{8}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $-\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{13}{16}$ |  |  |  |\n\nSo, the coordinates of the predicted new point are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2, \\\\ {y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p}=\\frac{1}{8}+\\frac{13}{16}=\\frac{2}{16}+\\frac{13}{16}=\\frac{15}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\frac{7}{8}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $-\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{13}{16}$ | $\\frac{15}{16}$ |  |  |\n\nHence, the slope at this predicted point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ={x}_{\\text{new}}-{y}_{p}-1 \\\\  & =2-\\frac{15}{16}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\frac{7}{8}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $-\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{13}{16}$ | $\\frac{15}{16}$ | $\\frac{1}{16}$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(5\\cdot \\frac{1}{16}+8\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{8})-(-2))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot (\\frac{21}{16}) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{64}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\frac{7}{8}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $-\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{13}{16}$ | $\\frac{15}{16}$ | $\\frac{1}{16}$ | $\\frac{7}{64}$ |\n\nThe value of $x$ in the next row is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=2.$ To get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $Δy$ to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}+\\frac{7}{64} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{64}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\frac{7}{8}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $-\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{13}{16}$ | $\\frac{15}{16}$ | $\\frac{1}{16}$ | $\\frac{7}{64}$ |\n| $2$ | $\\frac{15}{64}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conlude that $y(2)≈\\frac{15}{64}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305138 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b07de68-fffa-5655-8c46-6fd4367cb69c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{50}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303213}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b07de68-fffa-5655-8c46-6fd4367cb69c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{50}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303213} :answer-field/id #uuid "19d1eaf7-2e9c-50a8-9670-2515f953a6a9" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304141} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fce6f1f1-0472-5d7b-a14c-d3903d67c39b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{50}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304041}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fce6f1f1-0472-5d7b-a14c-d3903d67c39b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{50}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304041} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9e78988-2e99-5e74-948e-76be9ef86111" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304710}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bf9f1cd4-6e6f-5b7b-b4fe-71bb7082232f" :question/math-academy-id "q-332875" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n5\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-10y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)-7\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x).\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{2x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=A{e}^{2x}\\,+\\,($\n{{field-1}}\n$)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\,+\\,($\n{{field-2}}\n$)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)$" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n5\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}-10{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)-7\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x).$ So, we assume the particular solution to be \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n5(-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x))-10(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x))=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)-7\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x).\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(20\\beta -10\\alpha )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+(-20\\alpha -10\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)-7\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x).\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}20\\beta -10\\alpha &=3\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)) \\\\ -20\\alpha -10\\beta &=-7\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{11}{50},\\beta =\\frac{13}{50}.$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{11}{50}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+\\frac{13}{50}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x).\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{2x}+(\\frac{11}{50})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+(\\frac{13}{50})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305139 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54bc9c1e-5a31-5d5a-a1e5-08bb495ca020" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{19}{52}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302982}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54bc9c1e-5a31-5d5a-a1e5-08bb495ca020" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{19}{52}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302982} :answer-field/id #uuid "2fa5257d-356a-5b87-9cfe-1c65761fed54" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304193} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d7243d43-6ced-5078-8e54-65555b6271bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303801}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d7243d43-6ced-5078-8e54-65555b6271bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303801} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7b35234-8be4-52b1-af86-1ba0455dd06f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304667}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dce7b05c-a17f-5780-9764-3d1e2a74ae2c" :question/math-academy-id "q-332886" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-8y=-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{2x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=A{e}^{2x}\\,+\\,($\n{{field-1}}\n$)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,+\\,($\n{{field-2}}\n$)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)$" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n4\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}-8{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).$ So, we assume the particular solution to be \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n4(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))-8(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))=-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(12\\beta -8\\alpha )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+(-12\\alpha -8\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)=-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}12\\beta -8\\alpha &=-5\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)) \\\\ -12\\alpha -8\\beta &=2\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{1}{13},\\beta =-\\frac{19}{52}.$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{1}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)-\\frac{19}{52}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{2x}+(\\frac{1}{13})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+(-\\frac{19}{52})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305140 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "577ddd51-e3bd-5790-981c-b2f4d35144fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302989}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "577ddd51-e3bd-5790-981c-b2f4d35144fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302989} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2d672de-2a9b-5e87-8553-d2afe5b67ad3" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304651} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6eb7988a-9925-541c-a1be-439e5577b8e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{19}{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303132}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6eb7988a-9925-541c-a1be-439e5577b8e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{19}{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303132} :answer-field/id #uuid "f01e1a9b-a805-5e89-ae02-8bb83060bea3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304726}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "51ccf9c3-0db9-5b22-9dee-31cd44f9c3bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-332894" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+12y=-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+11\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-3x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=A{e}^{-3x}\\,+\\,($\n{{field-1}}\n$)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,+\\,($\n{{field-2}}\n$)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)$" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n4\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}+12{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+11\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).$ So, we assume the particular solution to be \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n4(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))+12(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))=-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+11\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(12\\beta +12\\alpha )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+(-12\\alpha +12\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)=-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+11\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}12\\alpha +12\\beta &=-8\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)) \\\\ -12\\alpha +12\\beta &=11\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{19}{24},\\beta =\\frac{1}{8}.$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{19}{24}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-3x}+(-\\frac{19}{24})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+(\\frac{1}{8})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308367 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c202ea7-be08-58b5-acd2-9a5d180907c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306187}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6c202ea7-be08-58b5-acd2-9a5d180907c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306187} :answer-field/id #uuid "238eea2d-8afd-5ec5-9f14-72e88a699aec" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307634} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d81ea3fc-50e1-546b-a338-7220adc095a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307155}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d81ea3fc-50e1-546b-a338-7220adc095a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307155} :answer-field/id #uuid "44744128-85b3-54f5-a1e9-5d773985daa7" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307733} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "607a64dc-0bf9-592a-9c6c-0414cd973799" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306081}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "607a64dc-0bf9-592a-9c6c-0414cd973799" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306081} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0b40cf5-8f74-5bc6-8dc3-e211e812acc1" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308083}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8e082c60-50f1-50ca-a231-91775e0a6957" :question/math-academy-id "q-333056" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2x-4y,\\,y(1)=2\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method. Given that the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{2},$ and that the first step has already been computed using the Runge-Kutta method, fill the missing entries in the table below, where ${y}_{p}$ denotes the predictor at each step.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-6$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $\\frac{7}{6}$ | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe predictor equation of the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method (ABM2) with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔ{y}_{p}=\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$ and\n\n- ${y}_{-1}^{′}=f({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1}).$\n\nThe initially predicted new $y$-value is then given by ${y}_{p}=y+Δ{y}_{p}.$\n\nWe're told that the first step is calculated using the Runge-Kutta Method, resulting in \n\n$$\ny(\\frac{3}{2})≈\\frac{7}{6}.\n$$\n\nNow, we'll continue from this point, using the ABM $2$-step method.\n\nFirst, we compute the value of ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2x-4y \\\\  & =2\\cdot (\\frac{3}{2})-4\\cdot (\\frac{7}{6}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-6$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $\\frac{7}{6}$ | $-\\frac{5}{3}$ |  |  |\n\nFrom the table, we see that the slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})=(1,2)$ is ${y}_{-1}^{′}=-6.$\n\nNext, we compute $Δ{y}_{p}$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot (-\\frac{5}{3})-(-6))\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this value to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-6$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $\\frac{7}{6}$ | $-\\frac{5}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |  |\n\nHence, according to the predictor equation, the initially predicted new $y$-value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{6}+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed table is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-6$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $\\frac{7}{6}$ | $-\\frac{5}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{17}{12}$ |"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308369 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0039aee6-98b7-59c0-8d03-5b2d50aa3e57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3(s+2)}{s(s-2)(s-5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305152}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0039aee6-98b7-59c0-8d03-5b2d50aa3e57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3(s+2)}{s(s-2)(s-5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305152} :answer-field/id #uuid "0377913c-e842-526c-a0d1-86f1c0379261" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307528}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e3c4c091-3098-5cdd-a98e-d6a345ec5978" :question/math-academy-id "q-333070" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=2{x}_{1}(t)+3{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=5{x}_{2}(t)+2,\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform $L$ to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a system with variables ${X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}.$ In terms of the parameter $s,$ the solution of the new system is\n\n${X}_{1}(s)=$ {{field-1}},$\\,$\n${X}_{2}(s)=\\frac{s+2}{s(s-5)}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the Laplace transforms of both sides of each equation in our system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}L\\{{x}_{1}^{′}(t)\\} &=L\\{2{x}_{1}(t)+3{x}_{2}(t)\\} \\\\ L\\{{x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\} &=L\\{5{x}_{2}(t)+2\\}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,\\,\n$$\n\nUsing the derivative property of the Laplace transform, we obtain the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}sL\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}-{x}_{1}(0) &=2L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}+3L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\} \\\\ sL\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}-{x}_{2}(0) &=5L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}+2L\\{1\\}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhich can be simplified to the following:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{1}(s)-0 &=2{X}_{1}(s)+3{X}_{2}(s) \\\\ s{X}_{2}(s)-1 &=5{X}_{2}(s)+\\frac{2}{s}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere ${X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\},$ and we have used the fact that $L\\{1\\}=\\frac{1}{s}.$\n\nNow, solving the second equation for ${X}_{2}(s),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{2}(s)-1 & =5{X}_{2}(s)+\\frac{2}{s} \\\\ (s-5){X}_{2}(s) & =1+\\frac{2}{s} \\\\ (s-5){X}_{2}(s) & =\\frac{s+2}{s} \\\\ {X}_{2}(s) & =\\frac{s+2}{s(s-5)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this in the first equation and solving for ${X}_{1}(s)$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{1}(s) & =2{X}_{1}(s)+3{X}_{2}(s) \\\\ (s-2){X}_{1}(s) & =3{X}_{2}(s) \\\\ (s-2){X}_{1}(s) & =3(\\frac{s+2}{s(s-5)}) \\\\ {X}_{1}(s) & =\\frac{3(s+2)}{s(s-5)(s-2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution of the new system in terms of parameter $s$ is \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}(s)=\\frac{3(s+2)}{s(s-2)(s-5)},\\,{X}_{2}(s)=\\frac{s+2}{s(s-5)}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308370 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d61152d-72ce-56c2-8f47-53ae4bc4ca1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{s}^{2}-9s+6}{s(s-4)(s+3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305270}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d61152d-72ce-56c2-8f47-53ae4bc4ca1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{s}^{2}-9s+6}{s(s-4)(s+3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305270} :answer-field/id #uuid "a218bca6-677e-5d3c-964c-6d26a25a6a98" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308038}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "674fd557-087f-5021-90ca-9f059df8fea3" :question/math-academy-id "q-333071" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=-3{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=4{x}_{2}(t)+6,\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform $L$ to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a system with variables ${X}_{1}(s)=L{{x}_{1}(t)}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L{{x}_{2}(t)}.$ In terms of the parameter $s,$ the solution of the new system is\n\n${X}_{1}(s)=$ {{field-1}},$\\,$\n${X}_{2}(s)=\\frac{6-s}{s(s-4)}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the Laplace transforms of both sides of each equation in our system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}L{{x}_{1}^{′}(t)} &=L{-3{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)} \\\\ L{{x}_{2}^{′}(t)} &=L{4{x}_{2}(t)+6}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,\\,\n$$\n\nUsing the derivative property of the Laplace transform, we obtain the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}sL{{x}_{1}(t)}-{x}_{1}(0) &=-3L{{x}_{1}(t)}+L{{x}_{2}(t)} \\\\ sL{{x}_{2}(t)}-{x}_{2}(0) &=4L{{x}_{2}(t)}+6L{1}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhich can be simplified to the following:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{1}(s)-2 &=-3{X}_{1}(s)+{X}_{2}(s) \\\\ s{X}_{2}(s)+1 &=4{X}_{2}(s)+\\frac{6}{s}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere ${X}_{1}(s)=L{{x}_{1}(t)}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L{{x}_{2}(t)},$ and we have used the fact that $L{1}=\\frac{1}{s}.$\n\nNow, solving the second equation for ${X}_{2}(s),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{2}(s)+1 & =4{X}_{2}(s)+\\frac{6}{s} \\\\ (s-4){X}_{2}(s) & =-1+\\frac{6}{s} \\\\ (s-4){X}_{2}(s) & =\\frac{-s+6}{s} \\\\ {X}_{2}(s) & =\\frac{6-s}{s(s-4)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this in the first equation and solving for ${X}_{1}(s)$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{1}(s)-2 & =-3{X}_{1}(s)+{X}_{2}(s) \\\\ (s+3){X}_{1}(s) & =2+{X}_{2}(s) \\\\ (s+3){X}_{1}(s) & =2+(\\frac{6-s}{s(s-4)}) \\\\ (s+3){X}_{1}(s) & =\\frac{2s(s-4)+6-s}{s(s-4)} \\\\ (s+3){X}_{1}(s) & =\\frac{2({s}^{2}-4s)+6-s}{s(s-4)} \\\\ (s+3){X}_{1}(s) & =\\frac{2{s}^{2}-8s+6-s}{s(s-4)} \\\\ (s+3){X}_{1}(s) & =\\frac{2{s}^{2}-9s+6}{s(s-4)} \\\\ {X}_{1}(s) & =\\frac{2{s}^{2}-9s+6}{s(s-4)(s+3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution of the new system in terms of parameter $s$ is \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}(s)=\\frac{2{s}^{2}-9s+6}{s(s-4)(s+3)},\\,{X}_{2}(s)=\\frac{6-s}{s(s-4)}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308371 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "847ba49d-4395-5042-91bf-3d6b21b87fbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{s}^{2}-s+1}{s(s-3)(s-4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306412}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "847ba49d-4395-5042-91bf-3d6b21b87fbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{s}^{2}-s+1}{s(s-3)(s-4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306412} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5eac305-981a-52bc-8d2d-b02f36abb75f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308053}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "771c46b3-1f61-5283-9850-fe39ec331e05" :question/math-academy-id "q-333073" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=4{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=3{x}_{2}(t)+1,\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform $L$ to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a system with variables ${X}_{1}(s)=L{{x}_{1}(t)}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L{{x}_{2}(t)}.$ In terms of the parameter $s,$ the solution of the new system is\n\n${X}_{1}(s)=$ {{field-1}},$\\,$\n${X}_{2}(s)=\\frac{2s+1}{s(s-3)}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the Laplace transforms of both sides of each equation in our system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}L{{x}_{1}^{′}(t)} &=L{4{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)} \\\\ L{{x}_{2}^{′}(t)} &=L{3{x}_{2}(t)+1}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,\\,\n$$\n\nUsing the derivative property of the Laplace transform, we obtain the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}sL{{x}_{1}(t)}-{x}_{1}(0) &=4L{{x}_{1}(t)}+L{{x}_{2}(t)} \\\\ sL{{x}_{2}(t)}-{x}_{2}(0) &=3L{{x}_{2}(t)}+L{1}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhich can be simplified to the following:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{1}(s)-1 &=4{X}_{1}(s)+{X}_{2}(s) \\\\ s{X}_{2}(s)-2 &=3{X}_{2}(s)+\\frac{1}{s}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere ${X}_{1}(s)=L{{x}_{1}(t)}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L{{x}_{2}(t)},$ and we have used the fact that $L{1}=\\frac{1}{s}.$\n\nNow, solving the second equation for ${X}_{2}(s),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{2}(s)-2 & =3{X}_{2}(s)+\\frac{1}{s} \\\\ (s-3){X}_{2}(s) & =2+\\frac{1}{s} \\\\ (s-3){X}_{2}(s) & =\\frac{2s+1}{s} \\\\ {X}_{2}(s) & =\\frac{2s+1}{s(s-3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this in the first equation and solving for ${X}_{1}(s)$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{1}(s)-1 & =4{X}_{1}(s)+{X}_{2}(s) \\\\ (s-4){X}_{1}(s) & =1+{X}_{2}(s) \\\\ (s-4){X}_{1}(s) & =1+(\\frac{2s+1}{s(s-3)}) \\\\ (s-4){X}_{1}(s) & =\\frac{s(s-3)+2s+1}{s(s-3)} \\\\ (s-4){X}_{1}(s) & =\\frac{{s}^{2}-3s+2s+1}{s(s-3)} \\\\ (s-4){X}_{1}(s) & =\\frac{{s}^{2}-s+1}{s(s-3)} \\\\ {X}_{1}(s) & =\\frac{{s}^{2}-s+1}{s(s-3)(s-4)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution of the new system in terms of parameter $s$ is \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}(s)=\\frac{{s}^{2}-s+1}{s(s-3)(s-4)},\\,{X}_{2}(s)=\\frac{2s+1}{s(s-3)}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308372 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4dfda933-44a9-53c0-a529-bc30371a2704" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305908}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4dfda933-44a9-53c0-a529-bc30371a2704" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305908} :answer-field/id #uuid "a91bd023-7cd3-5a0c-98f2-8167f0fcad3c" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308061} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a524255-dae4-5aa0-9476-f6bab4f52cec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305870}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a524255-dae4-5aa0-9476-f6bab4f52cec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305870} :answer-field/id #uuid "afd3094d-0605-5259-b346-971d5dc17d19" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308080} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab9b2a0f-0bca-52d2-84cc-d955b4917942" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306771}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab9b2a0f-0bca-52d2-84cc-d955b4917942" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306771} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6d50392-15f7-534f-9125-60156f7dacbd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308195}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "864c8c28-6fd7-5ccf-aa5a-86f2b52dc73a" :question/math-academy-id "q-333076" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=4x-2y-2,\\,y(1)=0\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method. Given that the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{4},$ and that the first step has already been computed using the Runge-Kutta method, fill the missing entries in the table below, where ${y}_{p}$ denotes the predictor at each step.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $2$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\frac{5}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe predictor equation of the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method (ABM2) with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔ{y}_{p}=\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$ and\n\n- ${y}_{-1}^{′}=f({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1}).$\n\nThe initially predicted new $y$-value is then given by ${y}_{p}=y+Δ{y}_{p}.$\n\nWe're told that the first step is calculated using the Runge-Kutta Method, resulting in \n\n$$\ny(\\frac{5}{4})≈\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we'll continue from this point, using the ABM $2$-step method.\n\nFirst, we compute the value of ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =4x-2y-2 \\\\  & =4\\cdot (\\frac{5}{4})-2\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2})-2 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $2$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\frac{5}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $2$ |  |  |\n\nFrom the table, we see that the slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})=(1,0)$ is ${y}_{-1}^{′}=2.$\n\nNext, we compute $Δ{y}_{p}$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot 2-2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this value to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $2$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\frac{5}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $2$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |\n\nHence, according to the predictor equation, the initially predicted new $y$-value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed table is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $2$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\frac{5}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $2$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ |"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308373 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ef4f2a86-9152-5040-8bd5-02b7ecc0fc9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307348}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef4f2a86-9152-5040-8bd5-02b7ecc0fc9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307348} :answer-field/id #uuid "b26f362f-68da-55de-88a3-cd8067ec80af" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308087} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16abc924-5915-58fe-aca7-a003748f3a5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305357}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16abc924-5915-58fe-aca7-a003748f3a5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305357} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3905f48-1ce9-5d06-bf84-0c16e1b43d85" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308089} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "854e06fb-3869-5e42-92b3-40c3db54718a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306421}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "854e06fb-3869-5e42-92b3-40c3db54718a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306421} :answer-field/id #uuid "c05f1757-d6d6-5d8c-abc4-9f15013210c5" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308126}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8d6b5b62-8ca3-5578-920e-608698061862" :question/math-academy-id "q-333077" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=4x-2y,\\,y(0)=-1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method. Given that the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{4},$ and that the first step has already been computed using the Runge-Kutta method, fill the missing entries in the table below, where ${y}_{p}$ denotes the predictor at each step.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe predictor equation of the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method (ABM2) with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔ{y}_{p}=\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$ and\n\n- ${y}_{-1}^{′}=f({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1}).$\n\nThe initially predicted new $y$-value is then given by ${y}_{p}=y+Δ{y}_{p}.$\n\nWe're told that the first step is calculated using the Runge-Kutta Method, resulting in \n\n$$\ny(\\frac{1}{4})≈-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we'll continue from this point, using the ABM $2$-step method.\n\nFirst, we compute the value of ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =4x-2y \\\\  & =4\\cdot (\\frac{1}{4})-2\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $2$ |  |  |\n\nFrom the table, we see that the slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})=(0,-1)$ is ${y}_{-1}^{′}=2.$\n\nNext, we compute $Δ{y}_{p}$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot 2-2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this value to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $2$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |\n\nHence, according to the predictor equation, the initially predicted new $y$-value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed table is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $2$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $0$ |"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308418 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ed2c971-651e-5d56-ada2-84bd3cb100d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305597} {:answer/id #uuid "3c166efe-ab22-53e7-b8cf-6a31dc4bf04e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305726} {:answer/id #uuid "9093d7cc-1ee0-5ba0-a467-0a9a86e3d62a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306522}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3c166efe-ab22-53e7-b8cf-6a31dc4bf04e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305726} :answer-field/id #uuid "053a1d32-1848-5017-9355-a805ad738f60" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307539} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56d17fe4-1972-58a4-aaf1-f0ed6976f7a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305991} {:answer/id #uuid "5f6ab164-1c7a-580a-9c14-74f58a7e7f0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306069} {:answer/id #uuid "b4643b1c-12a4-5e0e-88ba-fa77c45a3b3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306842}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "56d17fe4-1972-58a4-aaf1-f0ed6976f7a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305991} :answer-field/id #uuid "28bafe18-37f0-5fb0-98d8-df878247e043" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307650} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "412651a7-361a-553a-93e8-958aa7f394f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305793} {:answer/id #uuid "60a59f91-5da8-54e0-9534-06c7466710b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306082} {:answer/id #uuid "fa158e8f-6e8c-5999-b041-d4371db07b75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307469}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60a59f91-5da8-54e0-9534-06c7466710b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306082} :answer-field/id #uuid "41a2a9e0-218a-52ae-8da4-4a6ba530f33c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307723} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1053e4e0-6f48-59b4-942e-283a7a924d51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305301} {:answer/id #uuid "36f3dbb9-1dbf-5545-9700-4d0fec18877c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305682} {:answer/id #uuid "ddbf2f2a-7d63-5084-af0f-96a19808cda4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307206}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1053e4e0-6f48-59b4-942e-283a7a924d51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305301} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f8787d8-d9f7-592d-87d9-a78344009511" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307973}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cfae395a-5327-5bd0-a343-e2d83faa338d" :question/math-academy-id "q-333254" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-(x+1)(y+3),\\,y(0)=0\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the $2$-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method. Given that the step size is $Δx=1,$ and that one preliminary step has already been computed using the Runge-Kutta method, complete the next row of the table below.\n\n*Hint: The corrector equation of the two-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method is defined as*\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\n*where ${y}_{p}$ denotes the predictor at the current step, and ${y}_{-1}^{′}$ is the estimated slope at the previous point.* \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $-3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\frac{9}{4}$ |\n| $1$ | $-\\frac{9}{4}$ | $-\\frac{3}{2}$ | $-\\frac{3}{4}$ | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |\n\nTherefore, $y(2)≈$ {{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe $2$-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton (ABM2) Method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{-1}^{′}=f({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point (${x}_{-1},{y}_{-1}),$ and\n\n- ${y}_{p}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nFrom the second row of the partially filled table, we have that $y(1)≈-\\frac{9}{4},$ where the predicted $y$-increment is $Δ{y}_{p}=-\\frac{3}{4}.$\n\nSo, the coordinates of the predicted new point given by the predictor equation are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2, \\\\ {y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p}=-\\frac{9}{4}+(-\\frac{3}{4})=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $-3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\frac{9}{4}$ |\n| $1$ | $-\\frac{9}{4}$ | $-\\frac{3}{2}$ | $-\\frac{3}{4}$ | $-3$ |  |  |\n\nHence, the slope at this predicted point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =-({x}_{\\text{new}}+1)({y}_{p}+3) \\\\  & =-(2+1)(-3+3) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $-3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\frac{9}{4}$ |\n| $1$ | $-\\frac{9}{4}$ | $-\\frac{3}{2}$ | $-\\frac{3}{4}$ | $-3$ | $0$ |  |\n\nAlso from the table, we note the following:\n\n- From the second row, the slope at the current point $(x,y)=(1,-\\frac{9}{4})$ is ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\n- From the first row, the slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})=(0,0)$ is ${y}_{-1}^{′}=-3.$\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(5\\cdot 0+8\\cdot (-\\frac{3}{2})-(-3))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot (-9) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $-3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\frac{9}{4}$ |\n| $1$ | $-\\frac{9}{4}$ | $-\\frac{3}{2}$ | $-\\frac{3}{4}$ | $-3$ | $0$ | $-\\frac{3}{4}$ |\n\nThe value of $x$ in the next row is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=2.$ To get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $Δy$ to $y:$\n\n$$\n{y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy=-\\frac{9}{4}+(-\\frac{3}{4})=-3\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $-3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\frac{9}{4}$ |\n| $1$ | $-\\frac{9}{4}$ | $-\\frac{3}{2}$ | $-\\frac{3}{4}$ | $-3$ | $0$ | $-\\frac{3}{4}$ |\n| $2$ | $-3$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2)≈-3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308444 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02b7a747-95ef-5439-8a0d-a5d0bc972bf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+\\frac{2}{{t}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305178} {:answer/id #uuid "071d818f-582a-511b-8e1a-da0e02e5e527" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+1-\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305217} {:answer/id #uuid "39c46305-4821-5d35-b86b-257d73a82b77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+1+\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305701} {:answer/id #uuid "60474004-6f0b-518e-bd0a-d1fd73598741" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+2-\\frac{2}{{t}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306080} {:answer/id #uuid "79db6f46-1f9b-5903-82ac-d8484423c92c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306313}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60474004-6f0b-518e-bd0a-d1fd73598741" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+2-\\frac{2}{{t}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306080} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7db7aa7-b12e-53b6-82a4-b2d5f20ca566" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308196}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8a9de11b-dc85-5ae6-9285-a0d6cc055460" :question/math-academy-id "q-33336" :question/problem "Find $(f\\cdot g)^{′}(t)$, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,i+\\frac{1}{t}\\,j+t\\,k \\\\ g(t) & =t\\,i+\\frac{1}{t}\\,j+\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the dot product rule, we can write:\n\n$$\n(f\\cdot g)^{′}(t)={f}^{′}(t)\\cdot g(t)+f(t)\\cdot {g}^{′}(t)\n$$\n\nLet's now find the derivatives of each function.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{t})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\,i+(-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}})\\,j+1\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\,i-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\,j+k \\\\ {g}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{t})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)\\,k \\\\  & =1\\,i+(-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}})\\,j+\\frac{1}{t}\\,k \\\\  & =i-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\,j+\\frac{1}{t}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f\\cdot g)^{′}(t) & ={f}^{′}(t)\\cdot g(t)+f(t)\\cdot {g}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{t}\\,i-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\,j+k)\\cdot (t\\,i+\\frac{1}{t}\\,j+\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,k)+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,i+\\frac{1}{t}\\,j+t\\,k)\\cdot (i-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\,j+\\frac{1}{t}\\,k) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{t}\\cdot t-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{t}+1\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\cdot 1+\\frac{1}{t}(-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}})+t\\cdot \\frac{1}{t}) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+\\operatorname{ln}⁡t-\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}}+1 \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+2-\\frac{2}{{t}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308447 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69c024c8-cbcc-58a2-939e-a40af31a54a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306160} {:answer/id #uuid "a2372d5c-536a-5236-bac9-dfd679209e9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306674} {:answer/id #uuid "ff097213-fe54-52b5-99e0-b87fddae6322" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307505}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff097213-fe54-52b5-99e0-b87fddae6322" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307505} :answer-field/id #uuid "0de85b24-4dcb-5394-82d5-69aa9a855c0f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307572} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c4066d9-710b-5013-9774-538b0c0f86a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{128}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305257} {:answer/id #uuid "1d8ce066-1dc3-57fd-a2c2-1cfc803a719a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{218}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305428} {:answ