:answer/id #uuid "87f3ba9f-1a2b-52af-936d-67eb445e38a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306449} :answer-field/id #uuid "487685ef-8742-5813-b2d6-f9e3fb8d5e60" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307750} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3606fdd2-ca33-5638-a030-0d3bd25d89fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305671} {:answer/id #uuid "5e38cd68-9d4b-558e-9792-a156c89b775d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306056} {:answer/id #uuid "fd1f6575-bcdc-53b6-9305-a3259ed83b2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307491}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e38cd68-9d4b-558e-9792-a156c89b775d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306056} :answer-field/id #uuid "55d3b770-d5b4-5b3f-b8f0-e0faa47c6574" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307783} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03ff0b66-8902-5813-a9a1-67be227aad94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{21}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305188} {:answer/id #uuid "1f76106f-cb78-5786-89dd-2544055c0706" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305456} {:answer/id #uuid "59c31e33-294d-58b8-826f-8c7231574bc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306010}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "03ff0b66-8902-5813-a9a1-67be227aad94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{21}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305188} :answer-field/id #uuid "86a45c69-d0ad-5866-907b-58035013235b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307941}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "50ec2ab5-76b6-51aa-84ab-6c9b886fff22" :question/math-academy-id "q-333375" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=3{x}^{2}-2y,\\,y(1)=0\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method. Given that the step size is $Δx=1,$ and that one preliminary step has already been computed using the Runge-Kutta method, complete the next row of the table below.\n\n*Hint: The corrector equation of the two-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method is defined as*\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\n*where ${y}_{p}$ denotes the predictor at the current step, and ${y}_{-1}^{′}$ is the estimated slope at the previous point.* \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{15}{4}$ |\n| $2$ | $\\frac{15}{4}$ | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} | {{field-5}} |\n\nTherefore, $y(3)≈$\n  {{field-6}}." :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe $2$-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton (ABM2) method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{-1}^{′}=f({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point (${x}_{-1},{y}_{-1}),$ and\n\n- ${y}_{p}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nWe're told that the first step is calculated using the Runge-Kutta Method, resulting in \n\n$$\ny(2)≈\\frac{15}{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, we'll continue from this point, using the $2$-step ABM method.\n\nFirst, we compute the value of ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =3{x}^{2}-2y \\\\  & =3({2}^{2})-2(\\frac{15}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{15}{4}$ |\n| $2$ | $\\frac{15}{4}$ | $\\frac{9}{2}$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nFrom the first row of the table, we see that the slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})=(1,0)$ is ${y}_{-1}^{′}=3.$\n\nNext, we compute $Δ{y}_{p}$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot \\frac{9}{2}-3)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{21}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{15}{4}$ |\n| $2$ | $\\frac{15}{4}$ | $\\frac{9}{2}$ | $\\frac{21}{4}$ |  |  |  |\n\nSo, the coordinates of the predicted new point are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+1=3, \\\\ {y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p}=\\frac{15}{4}+\\frac{21}{4}=9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{15}{4}$ |\n| $2$ | $\\frac{15}{4}$ | $\\frac{9}{2}$ | $\\frac{21}{4}$ | $9$ |  |  |\n\nHence, the slope at this predicted point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =3{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}-2{y}_{p} \\\\  & =3({3}^{2})-2(9) \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{15}{4}$ |\n| $2$ | $\\frac{15}{4}$ | $\\frac{9}{2}$ | $\\frac{21}{4}$ | $9$ | $9$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(5\\cdot 9+8\\cdot \\frac{9}{2}-3)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot 78 \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{15}{4}$ |\n| $2$ | $\\frac{15}{4}$ | $\\frac{9}{2}$ | $\\frac{21}{4}$ | $9$ | $9$ | $\\frac{13}{2}$ |\n\nThe value of $x$ in the next row is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=3.$ To get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $Δy$ to $y:$\n\n$$\n{y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy=\\frac{15}{4}+\\frac{13}{2}=\\frac{41}{4}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{15}{4}$ |\n| $2$ | $\\frac{15}{4}$ | $\\frac{9}{2}$ | $\\frac{21}{4}$ | $9$ | $9$ | $\\frac{13}{2}$ |\n| $3$ | $\\frac{41}{4}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(3)≈\\frac{41}{4}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308458 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4232283d-ef4f-5093-9603-9b055cd7601f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x+Δx}-(\\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\cdot Δx)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305804} {:answer/id #uuid "45fd93c8-6f87-5160-8972-b02bf9a60f1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x+Δx}-\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305836} {:answer/id #uuid "5b41bf73-dc12-5668-a7ee-168afe51266a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x+Δx}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306027} {:answer/id #uuid "636445b1-b341-55b6-98e4-12e40e6470dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306114} {:answer/id #uuid "cf509c43-1335-5024-952d-2092c9886d20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\cdot Δx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307064}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4232283d-ef4f-5093-9603-9b055cd7601f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x+Δx}-(\\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\cdot Δx)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305804} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2f0f3ad-6bff-586d-b7b8-40dff6f4b80c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308133}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "10354088-c0e4-5ab7-aa11-decc50fd2a44" :question/math-academy-id "q-333498" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to approximate the solution to an initial value problem using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf the exact solution is ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)=\\frac{1}{x},$ find an expression for the local truncation error for the step from $x$ to ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx.$" :question/worked-solution "The local truncation error (LTE) is the error made in a single step of a numerical method, when the step begins from an exact value. The LTE of a numerical method at step $n$ from $x$ with exact value ${y}_{\\text{exact}}$ to ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{n} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-({y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)+Δy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that the exact solution of the initial value problem is ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)=\\frac{1}{x}.$ Now, let's find the $y$-increment given by the numerical method.\n\nFirst, the slope at a step starting at $x$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{exact}}^{′}(x)={(\\frac{1}{x})}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the increment given by the numerical method is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}^{′}(x)\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the exact value at ${x}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}}) & =({y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)+Δx) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x+Δx}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the LTE for step $n$ starting at $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{n} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-({y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)+Δy) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x+Δx}-(\\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\cdot Δx)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]
